## 2. `AndroidManifest.xml`

Inside `<manifest>` (top-level, before `<application>`):

```xml
<!-- Agent SMS send capability. Runtime permission on API 23+.
     If you don't need send_sms, remove this and SendSmsExecutor will
     simply report PermissionDenied — the Brain can plan around it. -->
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SEND_SMS" />

<!-- Agent direct-call capability. Runtime permission on API 23+.
     If you don't need make_call, remove this. Without CALL_PHONE, the
     agent can still decompose a call into open_app("phone") + type +
     click via Accessibility-tier executors — slower but permissionless. -->
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CALL_PHONE" />
```

Inside `<application>`:

```xml
<service
    android:name="com.amar.vault.agent.perception.PerceptionService"
    android:exported="false"
    android:label="@string/perception_service_label"
    android:permission="android.permission.BIND_ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.accessibilityservice.AccessibilityService" />
    </intent-filter>
    <meta-data
        android:name="android.accessibilityservice"
        android:resource="@xml/perception_service_config" />
</service>
```

No `<uses-permission>` needed for the Accessibility service itself.

### Runtime permission requests

Both SEND_SMS and CALL_PHONE require runtime grants on API 23+. Add a permission
request flow (ActivityResultContracts.RequestMultiplePermissions or similar) to
a settings screen or first-launch onboarding. The agent will fail SMS/call
actions with [PermissionDenied] until these are granted, but all other actions
work regardless.

